#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:2
msgid "Issue Management"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:4
msgid ""
"Bugs, feature requests and other things we need to do are tracked as GitHub "
"issues. Issues can be of various types and in various states, and also "
"belong to milestones or not. This page is an attempt to document the current "
"practice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:10
msgid "Labels"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:12
msgid ""
"Issues without labels are undecided - that is, we don't yet know if it's a "
"bug, a configuration issue, a feature request or what. Issues that are "
"invalid for whatever reason are closed with a short explanation of why. "
"Examples include \"Duplicate of #123\", \"Discovered to be configuration "
"error\", \"Rendered moot by #123\" and so on. We don't use the \"invalid\" "
"or \"wontfix\" labels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:19
msgid "api"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:20
msgid ""
"Issues or pull requests that change public APIs (config, REST). Used to "
"highlight these specifically in the release notes, and ensure we don't miss "
"to bump the minor version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:24
msgid "bug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:25
msgid ""
"A problem with current functionality, as opposed to missing functionality "
"(enhancement)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:28
msgid "build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:29
msgid ""
"Issues caused by or requiring changes to the build system (scripts or Docker "
"image)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:32
msgid "dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:33
msgid "Pull requests that update a dependency file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:35
msgid "documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:36
msgid "Issues related to the documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:38
msgid "enhancement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:39
msgid ""
"New features or improvements of some kind, as opposed to a problem (bug)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:42
msgid "frozen-due-to-age"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:43
msgid ""
"Set automatically on issues when they have been closed untouched for a long "
"time, together with being locked for discussion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:46
msgid "good first issue"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:47
msgid ""
"Good starting points for new contributors, contained in scope and size, and "
"clear about what is the desired outcome."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:50
msgid "needs-triage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:51
msgid "New issues needed to be validated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:53
msgid "not-our-bug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:54
msgid ""
"Rare and temporary label used only when we want to keep an issue open for "
"visibility, but the real problem is somewhere else."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:57
msgid "pr-merged"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:58
msgid ""
"Legacy label used in the past for pull requests merged to the main tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:61
msgid "protocol"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:62
msgid "Issues that require a change to the protocol."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:64
msgid "skip-changelog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:65
msgid ""
"Set on bugs that have never been in a released stable version, i.e. a bug "
"introduced in v1.8.0-rc.1 and fixed in v1.8.0-rc.2, and as such excluded "
"from the release notes for v1.8.0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:69
msgid "ui"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:70
msgid "Issues related to the graphical user interface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:72
msgid "waiting-for-info"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:73
msgid ""
"Issues that require more information from the author on how to reproduce."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:77
msgid "Milestones"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:79
msgid ""
"Each released version gets a milestone. Issues that are resolved and will be "
"released as that version get added to the milestone. The release notes are "
"based on the issues present in the milestone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:84
msgid "Assignee"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:86
msgid ""
"Users can be assigned to issues. We don't usually do so. Sometimes someone "
"assigns themself to an issue to indicate \"I'm working on this\" to avoid "
"others doing so too. It's not mandatory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:91
msgid "Locking"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:93
msgid ""
"We don't normally lock issues (prevent further discussion on them). There "
"are some exceptions though;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:96
msgid ""
"\"Popular\" issues that attract lots of \"me too\" and \"+1\" comments. "
"These are noise and annoy people with useless notifications via mail and in "
"the GitHub interface. Once the issue is clear and it suffers from this "
"symptom I may lock it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:101
msgid ""
"Contentious bikeshedding discussions. After two sides in a discussion have "
"clarified their points, there is no point arguing endlessly about it. As "
"above, this may get closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/issues.rst:105
msgid ""
"Duplicates. Once an issue has been identified as a duplicate of another "
"issue, it may be locked to prevent further discussion there. The intention "
"is to move the discussion to the other (referenced) issue, while someone "
"just doing a search and jumping on the first match might otherwise resurrect "
"discussion in the duplicate."
msgstr ""
